diagnosis_text:
The L3 leaf–spine fabric is healthy. End-to-end tests show the “middle” is forwarding normally: pc_0_0 ↔ pc_0_1 pings succeed both ways with 0% loss and ~2 ms RTT. Control-plane state is consistent with a working fabric: both leaves have established eBGP to both spines and are receiving the remote rack’s prefix. On leaf_router_0_0 (AS 65200), BGP neighbors 172.16.0.4 (spine_router_0_0) and 172.16.0.8 (spine_router_0_1) each show State/PfxRcd=1. On leaf_router_0_1 (AS 65201), neighbors 172.16.0.6 and 172.16.0.10 each show State/PfxRcd=1. This means each leaf has the opposite rack’s host route via both spines, providing ECMP and redundancy.

Spine adjacencies to the super-spine are up. One spine (spine_router_0_0) shows 0 prefixes received from the super-spine, while the other (spine_router_0_1) receives 2; however, the super-spine is not in the data path for traffic between these two racks, so this does not affect rack-to-rack reachability.

Mechanism: traffic from pc_0_0 to pc_0_1 is forwarded leaf→spine→leaf using routes learned via eBGP, which are present and active. Localization: all devices’ BGP sessions are established and exchanging needed host routes; no interface or daemon faults observed.

Root cause: None found in the network; the reported outage is not reproducible now.

Corrective fix: No changes required. If the issue was intermittent, enable BFD on eBGP sessions and review FRR and interface logs for prior flaps; continue to monitor. 
